ted problem is how to meet logging mandate. Audit uses logs. It solves implied problem of using logs for compliance. Might be 3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y). Or 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Logging mandate requires technical logging capability; audit is downstream. Probably applies to post-market monitoring aspect of Article12. I'd say applicability 3? Could be 4? \"do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem\". Stated problem includes meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Using logs for compliance reports is part of meeting mandate? The mandate requires logging capability, not analysis. But Recital 71 mentions availability of technical documentation and monitoring. Hmm.\n\nProbably applicability = 3: solves implied problem related to stated problem (making logs useful for compliance). Let's choose 3.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Establish quarterly audit cycle with dedicated team reviewing logs against Article12(2) criteria. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability: socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Internal audit is common. Score 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem. Options: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged.\n\nAudit protocol introduces process for logs but still treats compliance as logging practice. It's slightly paradigm stretching? Might be paradigm preserving: standard compliance practice. Audit is fairly standard. I'd say 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or 1? Common internal audit is standard. Let's pick 2.\n\nOriginality: degree not only rare but ingenious... Reference class is responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Post-market performance audit protocol is fairly common, mundane. Score 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nCheck evidence sentences one sentence each.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give ... first and then ... Probably list.\n\nWe'll output structured.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2260\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The protocol assumes logs already exist and only analyzes them, so it does not create the automatic logging capability mandated by Article 12(1) but helps use logs for traceability and post-market monitoring. That's one sentence.\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly addresses the implied need to make logged events useful for compliance with Article 12(2) criteria and post-market monitoring rather than the technical logging requirement itself. Good.\n\nImplementability evidence: Setting a quarterly review cycle with a dedicated team is a reasonably feasible organizational change that does not require new technology. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Internal audits for regulatory compliance are common business practice and raise no legal or social objections. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It adds a reporting process around existing logs but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice without weaving logging into R&D. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Quarterly log audits to produce compliance reports are a standard, expected response from regulated labs. Score 1.\n\nProvide scores.\n\nLet's output.",
