ompliance as logging practice.\n\nDefinition 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nIt seems it does change workflow to automate. Might be slightly paradigm stretching. I'd say 2.\n\nEvidence: The tool adds automated documentation generators to existing experiment tracking, leaving the core R&D process intact while formalizing compliance as an automated export step.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nIntegrating documentation generators into ML experiment tracking is reasonably feasible, some changes. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: Building automated export from existing tracking systems requires engineering effort but uses familiar tooling and data already captured in experiments.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nAutomated documentation is acceptable. 4.\n\nEvidence: Automating technical documentation generation aligns with industry norms and regulatory expectations without raising legal, ethical, or political concerns.\n\nNow output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale (applicability, effectiveness, originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability), in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nSo produce:\n\nid: P1915\n\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence each.\n\nLet's craft succinct sentences.\n\nFinal output.",
