4.\n\nEvidence: The framework directly automates capture of required timestamps, input matches and operator identities as mandated by Article 12.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = ...\n\nDirectly applies. Level 4.\n\nEvidence: It targets the lab's decision on how to meet Article 12 logging mandate for high-risk AI systems.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give ... Probably list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2898\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nProvide in order listed.\n\nEnsure evidence is one sentence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a reusable library and ledger but treats compliance as automated logging rather than changing how R&D relates to compliance. Score 2\n\noriginality_evidence: Automating regulatory telemetry with a shared library and immutable ledger is a common, mundane industry practice. Score 1\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building API hooks and integrating a shared library across pipelines requires engineering effort but is reasonably feasible. Score 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Automated compliance logging to an immutable ledger conforms to legal requirements and violates no norms. Score 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It directly automates capture of timestamps, input matches and operator identities required by Article 12. Score 4\n\napplicability_evidence: The framework addresses the lab's need to meet Article 12 record-keeping mandates for high-risk systems. Score 4\n\nReturn.\n\n",
