e and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nProposal auto-generates compliance reports. Article 12 requires logs of events relevant for identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring etc, specifically for high-risk systems point 1(a) Annex III: recording period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons involved... Does linking Git commits and dataset hashes to model weights solve logging mandate? Partially addresses technical documentation but not necessarily runtime logs of use events, start/end time, input data matches. Might be partial.\n\nIt shifts burden of record-keeping from scientists to automated infrastructure. Could contribute but may not fully cover required logging capabilities (runtime event logs). So effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial.\n\nEvidence: It would automate documentation lineage but does not guarantee capture of runtime use events, reference database checks, or human verification identities required by Article 12(3).\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nBuilding internal system scraping metadata from experiment trackers and version control, linking commits/dataset hashes to weights, immutable audit trail. That's some engineering work but feasible for R&D lab with devops. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nEvidence: Building a metadata pipeline requires integration with existing trackers and version control but uses standard tooling commonly available in labs.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms...\nAutomating compliance documentation is a