ording of events logs over lifetime, identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring. Proposal focuses on training lineage not operational logs. So maybe partially applies.\n\nAnchored: 4 = solves identified problem directly related (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal is about technical documentation, which is part of Recital 71 purpose but Article 12 is about logging events. Hmm.\n\nProbably applicability = 3? Solves implied problem related to stated problem: comprehensive technical documentation is related to record-keeping mandate. Might be directly related.\n\nLet's interpret: Stated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal meets Recital 71 requirements for comprehensive technical documentation without manual reporting. That's directly related to record-keeping requirement. Even if mismatch with Article 12 specifics, it's still about record-keeping.\n\nI think applicability = 4? It says do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Y = permanent audit trail of system evolution meets Recital 71 requirements. That is part of stated problem (meet logging mandate). Might be 4.\n\nBut effectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Will lineage system solve logging mandate? Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons. Proposal records dataset, model architecture, hyperparameters for training run. That's not operational logs. So it does NOT solve main problem; it addresses technical documentation but not logging of events. Could be partial solution.\n\nEffectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial. Yes contributes to traceability/documentation but not the event logging mandate.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. Using DVC/MLflow immutable ledger is common, acceptable. Score 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Integrate DVC or MLflow into CI/CD pipelines to hash inputs store metadata read-only database. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Might be 3. Could be 4 = easy low cost non-radical changes. Integrating MLflow into CI/CD is moderate effort. I'd say 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal introduces immutable ledger linking hashes to model versions. That's new tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. It doesn't change relationship between R&D and compliance beyond automating documentation. Slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements tools, formats, processes but still treats compliance as logging practice = 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious... \n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated lineage with DVC/MLflow is fairly common in ML ops. Not original. Common, mundane. Score 1? Could be 2 = interesting. I'd say common practice. Originality =1 = common mundane boring.\n\nLet's produce output with each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale (applicability, effectiveness, acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality), in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably output JSON or list? Probably just lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid P2773\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4? Might be 3. Let's think again about applicability anchor: solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). The stated problem includes meeting logging mandate. Proposal claims meets Recital 71 requirements for comprehensive technical documentation without manual reporting. That's a part of record-keeping requirement. So direct.\n\nI'll go with applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: lineage system records training provenance but does not capture operational event logs required by Article 12(1)-(3) such as use periods, input matches, or verification persons.\n\nEffectiveness = 3? If it will contribute partially. Could be 2 unreasonable unlikely to solve problem. Hmm. It is reasonable for technical documentation but not for logging mandate. So maybe effectiveness =3 contributes.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Using DVC/MLflow and immutable metadata storage is standard ML engineering practice that complies with EU norms and offends no sensibilities.\n\nAcceptability =4\n\nImplementability evidence: Integrating lineage tools into existing CI/CD requires pipeline changes, hashing and read-only storage setup which is reasonably feasible but not trivial low-cost.\n\nImplementability =3\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The system adds lineage tooling to R&D but keeps compliance framed as documentation logging rather than changing how research and compliance interrelate.\n\nParadigm_relatedness =2\n\nOriginality evidence: Automated dataset/model versioning with DVC/MLflow is a common, widely described MLOps practice for audit trails.\n\nOriginality =1\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nLet's output clearly.",
