nts but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nBudget ringfence is paradigm preserving? It doesn't change R&D or compliance relationship, just funding. It's standard compliance practice. So 1.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related...\nProblem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal addresses funding for logging infrastructure. Does it solve? It applies directly. Maybe 4? Solves resource allocation problem. Might be 3 solves implied problem? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate. Budget ensures resources. That's directly related. Probably applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill budget ringfence solve logging mandate? It enables maintenance but does not itself implement logs. Contributes to solution but partial. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, only partial.\n\nAlternatively effectiveness 3.\n\nLet's produce output.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating... Give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Likely output lines.\n\nWe'll produce JSON object with id and fields.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nAcceptability_evidence: \"Dedicating overhead funds to a compliance team is a common, legally neutral administrative practice.\" Score 4.\n\nImplementability_evidence: \"Reallocating a small percentage of overhead requires budget approval but is a reasonably feasible institutional measure.\" Score 3.\n\nOriginality_evidence: \"Budget ring-fencing for regulatory maintenance is a routine, mundane administrative response.\" Score 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness_evidence: \"The proposal tre