ate. Semantic logs are beyond mandatory. Might be implied problem? Could be 3 = solves implied problem related.\n\nThe proposal moves beyond simple event logging toward comprehensive map... It transforms compliance requirement into tool for scientific discovery. So applicability to meeting mandate? It does record alongside mandatory events, so it does meet? Actually it adds internal states. That is additional. Does it clearly apply to stated problem of how to meet logging mandate? Yes, it proposes a way to log. Might be 4.\n\nLet's decide: Applicability = 4? Evidence: The proposal designs telemetry that records mandatory Article 12 events plus internal states, directly addressing how the lab will meet the logging mandate. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nWill it solve stated problem? It would provide logs and also scientific discovery. But does it guarantee compliance? Recording internal model states alongside mandatory events should satisfy Article 12 logging requirement, and provides more. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? If you could do it, it would solve main problem? Possibly partial because semantic logs are extra, but meeting mandate is core. It may be reasonable and will contribute to solution, not fully solve? The mandate requires automatic recording of events logs. Semantic logs also record events. So it would solve.\n\nBut effectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Could argue it solves logging mandate plus adapt R&D. Seems reasonable.\n\nMaybe 3? It helps but is only partial solution because mandatory logs are timestamps etc., semantic logs may be extra overhead not required. Hmm.\n\nI'd say effectiveness = 3? Because it contributes to solution but also adds research overhead beyond compliance; still reasonable will contribute.\n\nThe anchor: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Might be 4.\n\nLet's pick 4 with evidence: If implemented, the semantic logs would capture all Article 12 required events plus richer data, fully satisfying traceability and post-market monitoring needs.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Developing internal telemetry for research is common. Acceptable. No violation. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Adding voluntary internal telemetry for model debugging does not violate EU AI Act norms and is socially acceptable in R&D labs. Score 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes =4\nSome changes, reasonably feasible=3\nSignificant change expensive/difficult=2\nTotally infeasible=1\n\nDesigning new telemetry probes that capture activation patterns and attention weights during high-risk triggers, correlating with timestamps. That's significant engineering, requires instrumentation, storage, privacy concerns. Not easy low cost. Might be significant change or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible =2? Could be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nGiven R&D lab, building probes is feasible but not trivial. Probably 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nLet's choose implementability =2.\n\nEvidence: Building probes to capture activation patterns and attention weights across high-risk systems requires substantial engineering, data storage, and integration work beyond standard logging.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious.\nNot expressed before AND ingenious =4\nUnusual interesting shows some imagination=3\nInteresting=2\nCommon mundane=1\n\nSemantic logging for AI behavioral analysis is unusual, interesting, shows some imagination. Not necessarily not expressed before? Might be 3.\n\nI'd give originality 3.\n\nEvidence: Combining mandatory regulatory logs with internal model telemetry for forensic analysis is unusual and shows imagination beyond typical compliance logging.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal transforms compliance requirement into powerful tool for scientific discovery and model debugging. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations, weaving logging into science. That's paradigm stretching? Or breaking?\n\nParadigm breaking requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does change relationship. Could be 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). 4 is paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship, or addresses larger problem. Might be 3.\n\nThe description says transforms compliance requirement into powerful tool for scientific discovery. That's changing relationship. Could be 3.\n\nLet's give paradigm_relatedness =3.\n\nEvidence: The proposal weaves mandatory logging into core R&D by using compliance data streams for model debugging and discovery, changing how compliance relates to research.\n\nNow output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer). Probably JSON.\n\nProvide entries:\n\nid\napplicability_evidence\napplicability\neffectiveness_evidence\neffectiveness\nacceptability_evidence\nacceptability\nimplementability_evidence\nimplementability\noriginality_evidence\noriginality\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence\nparadigm_relatedness\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's produce final.",
