ce but doesn't guarantee logs exist. It contributes. So effectiveness 3? Might be 4 if you think early warning solves compliance risk. Probably 3: contributes to solution.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\nBuilding internal diagnostic tool dashboard parsing unified logs and flags anomalies. Requires engineering, data pipeline. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nBuilding internal auditor is common, no violation. 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nA dashboard for log anomaly detection for AI compliance is fairly common/mundane. Might be interesting but not surprising. Probably 2 = interesting? Or 1 common? Many labs build monitoring dashboards. Could be 2 interesting.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal introduces tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new element tool, but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's 2.\n\nMaybe originality 1? Common mundane boring. Could be common. Hmm.\n\nLet's decide:\napplicability_evidence: The proposal builds an internal log analysis dashboard to flag risks and track human verification events, which directly addresses traceability and post-market monitoring implied by the logging mandate.\nScore 3? Let's pick 4? Let's re-evaluate anchor. \"solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of the stated problem)\". Stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Y = early warning system for post-market monitoring which is part of Article 12(2)(b). That's part of stated problem? Might be.\n\nI think many raters would say applicability 4. The proposal clearly applies. I'll go with 4.\n\nEffectiveness: reasonable and will contribute... maybe 3. It doesn't ensure logs are created, but helps use them. So 3.\n\nImplementability: some changes reasonably feasible. 3.\n\nAcceptability: common strategies violate no norms. 4.\n\nOriginality: interesting but not ingenious; log analysis dashboards are standard compliance practice. 2? Could be 1 common mundane. I'd say 2 = interesting. Might be 1 common mundane boring. Let's choose 2? Hmm.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: introduces tool but compliance remains logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching. 2.\n\nProvide output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably as JSON with fields.\n\nLet's output:\nid P1783\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\n",
