andate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal addresses technical documentation per Recital 71, which is about traceability and compliance, but Article 12 specifically mandates automatic recording of events/logs. Documentation pipeline may help with Recital 71 but not directly logging mandate? It uses training logs as input. Could be applicable.\n\nProbably applicability = 3? It solves implied problem of documentation burden related to compliance. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal explicitly says accelerates prototyping by automating tedious aspects of compliance documentation. That's related to mandate. I'd say 3: solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (reduce documentation burden). Or 4? Directly related? The mandate is logging, not documentation pipeline. Recital 71 mentions technical documentation and logs. So maybe directly related.\n\nI'll go with applicability = 3? Let's think: \"solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of the stated problem)\". The stated problem includes meet logging mandate. Does automated technical documentation pipeline solve logging mandate? It generates documentation from training logs, not create logs. Hmm.\n\nIt may have some benefit within special situation. Might be 2.\n\nBetter: applicability = 3: solves implied problem of compliance overhead related to logging mandate.\n\nI'll pick 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nAutomated documentation pipeline does not create logs; it processes existing logs into reports. It doesn't ensure automatic recording of events over lifetime as required by Article 12(1). So it contributes partially but does not solve logging mandate.\n\nThus effectiveness = 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (documentation part). Or 2? Might be unlikely to solve logging mandate.\n\nI'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nEvidence: It streamlines documentation required by Recital 71 but does not create the automatic event logs mandated by Article 12.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDeveloping a pipeline that parses hyperparameters, dataset versions, validation metrics into regulatory reports, linking Git commits. That's some engineering work but feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nEvidence: Building a parser and report generator requires integration effort but uses existing metadata sources.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon but does not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates laws\n\nThis is acceptable. 4.\n\nEvidence: Automating internal documentation complies with regulation and does not raise ethical or legal concerns.\n\nReturn JSON? Output format requested: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first and then <subscale> (integer). Echo id.\n\nProbably produce structured output.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P2941\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nMake sure order listed: paradigm_relatedness, originality, applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nEnsure exactly one sentence per evidence.\n\nProceed.",
