ship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis is a tool for logging. Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nUnified logging framework is common/mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2? Standardized library for compliance is fairly common idea. I'd say 1.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? Captures timestamps, input matches, user IDs into immutable ledger. Article 12 requires recording period of use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons involved in verification. This captures those. Should solve. So 4.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = intervention not stated\n\nDirectly applies. So 4.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nFormat? Probably JSON with fields. Instructions: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Probably output lines.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P2710\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nImpl