        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal P2464: Real-time Risk Signal Monitoring Dashboard. telemetry system designed to detect and flag anomalous behaviors... transforms raw logs into actionable alerts... sidecar monitoring agent analyzes logs for predefined trigger patterns related to performance drift or error spikes. visualized centralized dashboard for R&D team. This fulfills regulatory requirement for identifying situations that may result in high-risk system failures.\n\nRate subscales:\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is dashboard for monitoring logs. That's basically logging practice, with tooling. Introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? It doesn't change relationship between R&D and compliance, just makes logging actionable. Probably 2.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nReal-time monitoring dashboard for logs is common. Might be interesting but common. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 = interesting? Telemetry sidecar is fairly standard. Likely 1.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates\n\nThis is acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. So 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nSidecar monitoring agent, dashboard - requires engineering effort. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Maybe 3? Could be easy? Probably 3. Not trivial low cost. So 3.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = ...\n\nStated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal fulfills requirement for identifying situations... Article 12(2)(a). So directly applies. 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable/unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nIt will help identify risk situations and provide logs. Does it fulfill logging mandate? It uses logs to detect, but does it ensure automatic recording of events over lifetime? It monitors logs but doesn't guarantee logging itself. Might be partial. It fulfills requirement for identifying situations... The requirement is to enable recording of events relevant for identifying situations. Having dashboard doesn't necessarily ensure logging capabilities per Article 12(3) specifics. Might contribute. Probably 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial. Could be 4? It claims fulfills regulatory requirement for identifying situations. If you could do it, it would solve main problem? The mandate is record-keeping, not monitoring dashboard. Dashboard is extra. Might be 3.\n\nLet's choose: paradigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces a monitoring dashboard and sidecar agent but treats compliance as logging practice without changing R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2\n\noriginality_evidence: Real-time log telemetry dashboards are a common industry practice for AI systems. Score 1\n\nacceptability_evidence: Monitoring logs for safety risks is socially, legally and politically unobjectionable. Score 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Deploying a sidecar agent and centralized visualization requires engineering changes but is reasonably feasible. Score 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly addresses Article 12(2)(a) requirement to identify risk situations via logs. Score 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It helps flag risks but does not by itself guarantee the mandated automatic recording and retention of all required log elements. Score 3\n\nReturn JSON? Instruction says echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably output format lines.\n\nWill produce structured.\n\n",
