 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Y = precise classification prevents over-engineering and ensures no bypass. That is part of problem of how to meet mandate.\n\nScore 4.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... Common, mundane, boring? Classification audit tool for regulatory compliance is common. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting? I think it's common practice. Might be 1.\n\nEvidence: Diagnostic questionnaires mapping projects to Annex III are standard compliance practice in regulated R&D labs.\n\nScore 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem. Options: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship. 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged.\n\nThis is standard classification tool, treats compliance as logging practice. So 1 = paradigm preserving.\n\nEvidence: The proposal keeps R&D workflow separate from compliance by adding a conventional gatekeeping questionnaire rather than integrating logging into science.\n\nScore 1.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Building an internal audit questionnaire with legal and technical leads is a normal, non-controversial compliance activity.\n\nScore 4.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Some changes reasonably feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy low cost? Building tool requires