        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rate proposal P1392.\n\nText: Unified Compliance Logging Framework. reusable middleware library standardizes event recording across all high-risk AI prototypes to ensure consistent traceability. build standardized wrapper around existing telemetry tools to automatically capture session durations, input data matches, and reference database versions across different model architectures. Ensures technical viability by preventing engineers wasting time building custom logging mechanisms.\n\nWe need subscales:\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nEvidence sentence then score.\n\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is build middleware library to standardize event recording, capture session durations, input data matches, reference database versions.\n\nEffectiveness: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? It addresses logging mandate: Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use, reference database, input data for which search led to match, identification of natural persons... The proposal captures session durations, input data matches, reference database versions. Might miss identification of natural persons involved in verification. But generally will solve main problem? Probably contributes to solution, partial. It standardizes logging. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial solution). Could be 4? It says reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Does it solve completely? Article 12(3) requires specific items for point 1(a) Annex III: recording period, reference database, input data for which search led to match, identification of natural persons. Proposal mentions session durations (period), input data matches, reference database versions. Missing identification of natural persons. Also logging events relevant for risk, post-market monitoring etc. Might be partial. So effectiveness 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related: do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes building unified logging framework directly addresses meeting logging mandate. So applicability 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is reusable middleware library standardizing event recording. That's introducing new tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements but still compliance as logging practice. Might be 2. Not changing relationship between R&D and compliance fundamentally, just making logging easier. So 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but also ingenious...\nCommon middleware for logging? Probably common, mundane. Standardized wrapper around telemetry. That's fairly common. Could be interesting? Might be 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting. I'd say common practice in software engineering: unified logging framework. Not rare. So originality 1.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nBuild standardized wrapper around existing telemetry tools to automatically capture... Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Could be 3. Not easy low cost? Might need some engineering effort but feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Maybe 4 = easy to implement at low cost, non-radical changes. Building middleware library may be moderate effort. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. Unified logging framework is acceptable. So 4.\n\nWe need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder: effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then score.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id. Provide for each subscale ... Could be simple lines.\n\nI'll output as:\n\nid: P1392\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The framework automates capture of session durations, input matches and reference database versions, directly addressing core Article 12 logging requirements for high-risk prototypes. That's effectiveness? Might be partial. Sentence should justify rating 3. Could say it contributes but does not cover all mandated items like identification of verifiers.\n\nBetter: It standardizes logging of key mandated data points but does not explicitly ensure capture of risk-related events, post-market monitoring data, or verifier identities required by Article 12.\n\nThat's one sentence.\n\napplicability_evidence: Building a reusable middleware to record events for high-risk AI prototypes directly applies to the lab's mandate to meet Article 12 record-keeping obligations. Score 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal introduces a new logging tool and process but still treats compliance as a separate logging practice rather than changing how R&D and compliance relate. Score 2.\n\noriginality_evidence: A standardized middleware wrapper for telemetry and compliance logging is a common engineering practice in regulated software development. Score 1.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Wrapping existing telemetry tools with a shared library is reasonably feasible with moderate engineering effort across model architectures. Score 3.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Standardizing internal logging to meet legal traceability requirements violates no norms and aligns with expected business practice. Score 4.\n\nEnsure scores integers.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
