e and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? It addresses only one sub-requirement (identification of persons). Logging mandate also requires automatic recording of events, logs, period of each use, reference database, input data for match. This proposal does not address logging technical capabilities, only governance registry. So it contributes partially.\n\nEffectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial solution). Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: It creates the human accountability trail required by Article12(3)(d) but does not provide automatic event logging or technical log capture for use periods or input data, so it is only partial.\n\nScore 3.\n\nnovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nAudit protocol with quarterly reviews and registry of authorized persons. Is that standard compliance? Likely standard governance process. It's paradigm preserving? Might be slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds a formal categorization and registry process but keeps compliance as separate administrative oversight rather than integrating logging into R&D.\n\nScore 2.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows