lution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\nProposal investigates lossy compression and intelligent triggering that only records critical state changes. However Article 12 requires logging of events relevant for identifying risk, post-market monitoring, monitoring operation, and for systems in point 1(a) Annex III minimum items including period of each use, reference database, input data match, identification of persons. Intelligent triggering that only records critical state changes may risk missing required logs. Lossy compression may compromise traceability. So effectiveness is questionable; it may contribute but not guarantee compliance. It will contribute to solving cost problem but may undermine logging requirement.\n\nEffectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution? Might be 3? Or 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. The proposal aims to minimize overhead, but lossy compression and selective recording could violate logging completeness required by Article 12. So it is unlikely to solve compliance problem; might even create non-compliance risk.\n\nProbably effectiveness = 2? Definition: unreasonable or unlikely to solve the problem (it probably will not work). Could argue it helps cost but not meet mandate. The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Reducing overhead helps meet mandate practically, but selective recording may fail legal requirement.\n\nI'd say effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Hmm.\n\nBetter read anchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Would lossy compression solve logging mandate? No, because mandate requires recording events. Lossy may lose information. So not solve.\n\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution. It contributes to cost reduction which helps implement logging. Could be partial.\n\nI'll choose 3.\n\nAcceptab